    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as stiffness/spasms/ hyperlordosis, positive antibodies such as anti-GAD, EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity, response to benzodiazepines",
      "quote": "Systemically, SPS is characterised by muscle spasms and rigidity as well as lumbar hyperlordosis and difficult ambulation.\n\nIt has a predilection for females\n5\nand case reports describe clinical onset ranging from 1 to 76 years of age.\n1–7\nThough epidemiological data on SPS is limited due to its rarity, the currently reported median age of symptom onset is 40 years.\n4 , 5\nWhile the understood cause is an autoimmune response to GAD,\n1–7\na small minority of SPS patients present without antiGAD antibodies.\n4–6\nSPS often presents with coexisting autoimmune conditions (such as type 1 diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease) and, less frequently, coexisting cancer (such as various carcinomas or non-Hodgkin lymphoma).\n5–7\nSymptomatic treatment of SPS includes benzodiazepines (most commonly diazepam) and baclofen.\n1 , 4–7\nImmunotherapy, namely intravenous immunoglobulin and azathioprine, has also been used with success,\n3–7\nas was demonstrated in the patient in this case.",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
